From 4ca7203c8cabe163961f5c069981f7c9fce7bcf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:55:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] camera: speed ramping, FPS changes during cDNG/MLV takes
 behind fps_ramp

setFps() refused every change while recording so video and audio kept one
timeline. Cinemate lets the rate move mid-take (set fps, set_fps_double,
_ramp_fps; shutter_a_sync_mode picks angle or exposure) and conforms in
post. Ported as an opt-in with the bookkeeping Core needs for it:

- CaptureSettings: fpsRampEnabled (persisted fps_ramp) and
  fpsRampKeepExposure (fps_ramp_keep_exposure). setFps() allows a change
  during a take when ramping is on and the format is cDNG or MLV; H.264,
  ProRes and still mode keep the old refusal. A ramp step is take-scoped:
  the UI hears it, the settings file does not, and the take's start rate is
  restored (and persisted) at stop. Keep-exposure keeps the operator's
  exposure across clamps to the frame (the IPA would shorten it silently)
  and returns to it on the way back down. One journal line per request and
  one per frame at which the sensor reached the requested period, with the
  lag in frames.
- Frame numbering: with ramping on, the recorder numbers frames by count
  instead of max(count, elapsed x start fps); every frame's FrameRate tag and
  RecRun timecode use the FPS the take started with, so the DNG sequence is
  one clip at that rate and a faster segment plays as slow motion. FreeRun
  timecode counts frames at that rate from the first frame's wall time
  (Metadata::timecodeWallClockUs) instead of each frame's own wall time,
  which would repeat or skip frame numbers through a ramped segment.
- Audio: AudioRecordingOptions::fpsRamp makes observeVideoFrame() end the
  WAV at the last frame's own FrameDuration instead of the next grid point at
  the start rate. The WAV stays real time; the picture does not, by design.
- Session: applyControls() reports each FPS change. A pending control rides
  the next requeued request behind the N-1 already queued, then DelayedControls
  writes it two frames ahead, so the new period is programmed ~N+2 frames
  after the request and shows in a timestamp delta ~N+3; for N+6 frames of
  the slower rate (at least 10, APERTAR_FPS_RAMP_GRACE_FRAMES) the capture-gap
  estimate and the phase lock sit out the switch. Changes during a take are
  stamped with the next frame index, paired with the frame whose period
  reached the request (skipped periods closed as superseded), and written to
  <clip>/fps-ramp.txt at stop with the applied frame's timecode at the clip
  rate and a stats line in the journal. Nothing is written when the medium
  is gone (T28).
- App: set_fps_ramp {enabled, shutter=angle|exposure}; ramp_fps {to, seconds}
  steps one fps per step on a helper thread (Cinemate's _ramp_fps) and stops
  at the mode's ceiling; a stepper started in a take only steps while that
  take runs (setFps takeOnly) and is stopped by record_start, record_stop and
  the failure path; state carries fps_ramp_stepping and fps_take_base.
- Review fixes: the grace window only grows; a change pairs no earlier than
  two frames after its stamp; the restore at stop also re-persists when the
  ramp came back to the start rate; keep-exposure applies only with ramping
  on and its nominal resets on an explicit shutter, a policy change or a
  preset; a shutdown during a ramp take writes fps-ramp.txt and restores;
  ramp_fps seconds clamped to 0..600; in a take, ramp_fps refuses up front with
  setFps's own messages when ramping is off or the take is H.264/ProRes/still; the session's recordingBaseFps()
  sits in its public section next to phaseLockStatus() (the 8f7dc39
  pattern; this branch has no friend declaration).
- On cinemate-port: the WAV end in observeVideoFrame() uses the latest
  frame's own duration (videoLastDurationUs_, frames can arrive out of
  order since d9f00d4), and fps-ramp.txt is written right after the
  timed raw-recorder stop (56d9137), before the sync at stop (0586273).

Host-checked: ApertarCoreApp.cpp, AudioRecordingSession.cpp, CdngEncoder.cpp,
LossyDngEncoder.cpp, MlvEncoder.cpp, WebControlPage.cpp pass g++
-fsyntax-only. CameraController.cpp needs libcamera and is not compiled here.

Rebased onto eb9cc39 (2026-10-28): Patrik's automatic Clear HDR switches
at the top of setFps (16-bit Clear HDR above 25 fps to 12-bit SDR, and
back at 25 or less) are guarded with !recording_: both reconfigure, so
they never run in a take; a ramp stays in the take's mode.

